1
00:00:18,324 --> 00:00:20,094
[♪ lively music playing]

2
00:00:24,469 --> 00:00:27,374
Can we light some candles
or something, you know?

3
00:00:27,375 --> 00:00:30,581
Make it a little more romantic.
Since this is a double date?

4
00:00:30,582 --> 00:00:33,153
- Ooh, that's a nice idea.
- [Susan] Thanks.

5
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:34,890
[Bret] I'd like to keep
the lights on.

6
00:00:34,891 --> 00:00:39,131
And maybe we should, you know,
move the party inside.

7
00:00:39,132 --> 00:00:41,135
[Thom] Why?

8
00:00:41,136 --> 00:00:43,541
The home invasions.
There was another
one last Sunday night.

9
00:00:43,542 --> 00:00:47,147
And the houses were dark
when the home invasions
happened?

10
00:00:47,148 --> 00:00:49,285
I mean,
that's why people broke in?

11
00:00:49,286 --> 00:00:52,424
I don't know, but I'm assuming
with the lights on,
it's a deterrent.

12
00:00:52,425 --> 00:00:53,460
A deterrent.

13
00:00:54,262 --> 00:00:56,366
- A deterrent.
- Whoever is doing it

14
00:00:56,367 --> 00:01:00,340
also steals people's pets,
their cats, their dogs.

15
00:01:00,341 --> 00:01:01,777
Uses them ritualistically.

16
00:01:01,778 --> 00:01:03,446
[stammers]
Do you remember, Susan,

17
00:01:03,447 --> 00:01:05,685
the school mascot
on the first day of school?

18
00:01:05,686 --> 00:01:08,323
- Cult.
- Another girl is missing, too.

19
00:01:08,324 --> 00:01:10,629
- Who?
- Julie Selwyn from Marymount.

20
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:13,433
It's been almost eight weeks
since she disappeared.

21
00:01:13,434 --> 00:01:15,270
Maybe she just ran away.

22
00:01:15,271 --> 00:01:18,544
Okay, guys, let's stop trying
to creep ourselves out.

23
00:01:18,545 --> 00:01:20,348
I wanna talk about the party.

24
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:24,422
- The party?
- Yes.

25
00:01:25,191 --> 00:01:27,428
I'm going to throw a party
for Robert Mallory.

26
00:01:28,330 --> 00:01:30,668
It's going to be at my house,

27
00:01:30,669 --> 00:01:32,939
and I think we should
all throw it together,
the four of us, to welcome him.

28
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:35,243
And we'll invite everyone
in our senior class.

29
00:01:35,244 --> 00:01:38,752
- [Thom] Totally awesome.
- Cool. Fun. Love it. [sniffs]

30
00:01:39,519 --> 00:01:40,589
Wait, why?

31
00:01:41,323 --> 00:01:43,226
Why throw him a party?

32
00:01:43,227 --> 00:01:45,363
I think we should
make him feel welcome.

33
00:01:45,364 --> 00:01:49,139
I think he had some hard times,
and I think he needs
to feel welcome.

34
00:01:50,642 --> 00:01:51,878
Hard times?

35
00:01:53,447 --> 00:01:55,217
What were the hard times?

36
00:01:55,585 --> 00:01:56,888
[♪ tense music playing]

37
00:02:00,294 --> 00:02:02,832
I was in Principal Croft's
office yesterday...

38
00:02:02,833 --> 00:02:05,905
He spent some time
in a developmental center
working on his...

39
00:02:05,906 --> 00:02:08,944
[Susan] Dr. Croft asked me
to throw this party.

40
00:02:08,945 --> 00:02:10,882
He said he was worried
about Robert

41
00:02:10,883 --> 00:02:13,587
and that my responsibility
as student body president

42
00:02:13,588 --> 00:02:16,426
was to make everyone feel
as welcome as possible.

43
00:02:16,427 --> 00:02:17,629
I totally agree.

44
00:02:18,097 --> 00:02:20,434
- It seems that...
- What?

45
00:02:20,435 --> 00:02:23,440
Robert was institutionalized.

46
00:02:23,441 --> 00:02:25,645
In an asylum?
You've got to be kidding me.

47
00:02:25,646 --> 00:02:28,617
Croft said it was more
of a developmental center.

48
00:02:28,618 --> 00:02:30,955
He was treated for depression
and some other issues.

49
00:02:30,956 --> 00:02:33,594
Great. So the guy's been
locked away in a loony bin.

50
00:02:33,595 --> 00:02:36,567
That's a little dramatic, Bret.
You always embellish
everything.

51
00:02:36,568 --> 00:02:39,405
- I'm a writer. It's my job.
- Look, no biggie.

52
00:02:39,406 --> 00:02:41,008
The guy went through some shit,

53
00:02:41,009 --> 00:02:45,283
his parents' divorce and
whatnot, his mother's death.

54
00:02:45,284 --> 00:02:46,453
I feel sorry for him.

55
00:02:48,390 --> 00:02:51,897
Robert Mallory needs a friend
and I intend to be one.

56
00:02:51,898 --> 00:02:53,399
- Me too.
- Me three.

57
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,571
[Debbie] Wanna toot, Thommy?

58
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:57,675
[Thom]
No thanks, Debs. I gotta get up
early tomorrow to practice.

59
00:02:57,676 --> 00:02:59,813
- [Debbie] So that'll
keep your body tight?
- I'm already tight.

60
00:02:59,814 --> 00:03:02,552
- I'm keeping it tight.
- You know what?
I feel like some red wine.

61
00:03:02,553 --> 00:03:04,689
- Anyone for a nice cab?
- Please.

62
00:03:04,690 --> 00:03:06,927
He's a liar, guys.

63
00:03:06,928 --> 00:03:09,465
He lied about seeing me
at the movie theater

64
00:03:09,466 --> 00:03:12,504
last summer during The Shining.
I do not trust him
and neither should you.

65
00:03:12,505 --> 00:03:15,611
Bret, it's just a party.

66
00:03:15,612 --> 00:03:17,514
No, Susan, it's a validation.

67
00:03:17,515 --> 00:03:20,453
Hey, guys, it's getting
a little tense in here.

68
00:03:20,454 --> 00:03:24,361
- Maybe we should hot-tub it.
- [gasps] Let's hot-tub it.
Come on.

69
00:03:24,362 --> 00:03:26,299
- Let's go to the hot tub.
Okay? Come on.
- [screams]

70
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:27,335
Come on. [chuckles]

71
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:29,506
[♪ playful music playing]

72
00:03:35,686 --> 00:03:39,259
♪ Jesus ♪

73
00:03:40,863 --> 00:03:43,032
♪ Another day goes by ♪

74
00:03:43,033 --> 00:03:44,269
[Debbie screams]

75
00:03:45,104 --> 00:03:47,008
[Debbie, Susan giggling]

76
00:03:50,849 --> 00:03:52,920
[no audible dialogue]

77
00:03:57,563 --> 00:04:02,437
[chuckles] Look,
we were in a mall. Remember?

78
00:04:02,438 --> 00:04:03,775
- [twig snaps]
- [Susan] No.

79
00:04:07,716 --> 00:04:08,751
[owl hooting]

80
00:04:10,522 --> 00:04:11,557
Oh, no.

81
00:04:11,991 --> 00:04:13,694
What's that? The killer?

82
00:04:13,695 --> 00:04:15,363
[Debbie, Susan laugh]

83
00:04:15,364 --> 00:04:16,399
[Thom laughs]

84
00:04:16,834 --> 00:04:19,072
Bret, please become a wino.

85
00:04:19,506 --> 00:04:22,679
You are so much more fun
when you drink. [chuckles]

86
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:25,517
Okay, so we'll do a Saturday.

87
00:04:25,518 --> 00:04:27,655
My house. Robert's party.

88
00:04:27,656 --> 00:04:29,960
- It's settled. Settled.
- It's settled.

89
00:04:29,961 --> 00:04:31,763
- What?
- Scheduled. [chuckles]

90
00:04:31,764 --> 00:04:33,934
Guys, just to state the facts.

91
00:04:33,935 --> 00:04:36,038
He followed me to the mall.

92
00:04:36,039 --> 00:04:40,615
That's funny because
he told me that you were
the one following him.

93
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:44,823
- How do you know?
Did you talk to him?
- [Susan chuckles]

94
00:04:48,497 --> 00:04:50,400
[♪ "Don't You Want Me" playing]

95
00:04:50,401 --> 00:04:52,739
- [Debbie gasps, chuckles]
- Oh, my God!

96
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:56,079
I love this song. [shrieking]

97
00:05:00,154 --> 00:05:01,556
[laughs]

98
00:05:01,557 --> 00:05:02,860
Turn it up!

99
00:05:04,496 --> 00:05:07,368
Whoo. Go, Susan.

100
00:05:09,707 --> 00:05:13,046
Are we getting a show? Whoo.

101
00:05:13,047 --> 00:05:15,818
Yes! Susan.

102
00:05:15,819 --> 00:05:17,922
Oh, Thom. You're in for it.
You're in for it.

103
00:05:17,923 --> 00:05:19,627
- Prepare.
- She's right behind me?

104
00:05:21,196 --> 00:05:23,902
[Debbie] Oh. [chuckles]

105
00:05:24,604 --> 00:05:28,877
♪ Now five years later on
You've got the world
At your feet ♪

106
00:05:28,878 --> 00:05:32,084
♪ Success has been
So easy for you ♪

107
00:05:32,085 --> 00:05:33,120
Come.

108
00:05:34,222 --> 00:05:36,359
Come on, give us choreography.

109
00:05:37,095 --> 00:05:38,430
[Susan giggling]

110
00:05:41,436 --> 00:05:44,710
♪ Don't, don't you want me? ♪

111
00:05:45,244 --> 00:05:49,653
♪ You know I can't believe it
When I hear that
You won't see me ♪

112
00:05:49,654 --> 00:05:52,926
♪ Don't, don't you want me? ♪

113
00:05:53,394 --> 00:05:57,835
♪ You know I don't believe you
When you say that
You don't need me ♪

114
00:05:57,836 --> 00:06:01,777
♪ It's much too late to find
You think you've changed
Your mind ♪

115
00:06:01,778 --> 00:06:06,052
♪ You'd better change it back
Or we will both be sorry ♪

116
00:06:06,053 --> 00:06:09,125
♪ Don't you want me, baby? ♪

117
00:06:10,494 --> 00:06:13,967
♪ Don't you want me? Oh ♪

118
00:06:13,968 --> 00:06:17,508
♪ Don't you want me, baby? ♪

119
00:06:18,678 --> 00:06:20,815
♪ Don't you want me? Oh ♪

120
00:06:20,816 --> 00:06:26,192
[gasps] Oh, Bret,
please get drunk every night.

121
00:06:26,961 --> 00:06:27,996
[moaning]

122
00:06:28,932 --> 00:06:30,467
[panting]

123
00:06:31,537 --> 00:06:33,272
[both moaning]

124
00:06:33,273 --> 00:06:35,009
[♪ song ends]

125
00:06:35,010 --> 00:06:36,212
[birds chirping]

126
00:06:37,883 --> 00:06:41,122
- [Debbie chuckling]
- [inhales sharply]

127
00:06:41,123 --> 00:06:42,491
Hi, sexy.

128
00:06:43,661 --> 00:06:45,497
You were amazing last night.

129
00:06:46,199 --> 00:06:48,070
I was, wasn't I?

130
00:06:49,172 --> 00:06:50,575
Matt called.

131
00:06:52,613 --> 00:06:54,015
- Who?
- Matt.

132
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:55,852
From school?

133
00:06:57,556 --> 00:06:59,091
What did he want?

134
00:06:59,092 --> 00:07:01,229
Oh, you were so knocked out,
I didn't wanna wake you.

135
00:07:02,699 --> 00:07:04,837
He said to stop calling.

136
00:07:05,405 --> 00:07:06,740
[♪ tense music playing]

137
00:07:06,741 --> 00:07:08,644
What's that supposed to mean?

138
00:07:08,645 --> 00:07:10,114
No idea. [chuckles]

139
00:07:12,753 --> 00:07:14,055
I'll see you tonight.

140
00:07:29,653 --> 00:07:32,124
[Bret] One of the hallmarks
of the early 1980s

141
00:07:32,125 --> 00:07:34,062
for a young person
in Los Angeles

142
00:07:34,496 --> 00:07:37,803
was you had to survive things
to become an adult.

143
00:07:39,807 --> 00:07:43,614
Real-life boogeymen
known as serial killers
were a threat.

144
00:07:43,615 --> 00:07:44,749
[siren wailing]

145
00:07:44,750 --> 00:07:46,219
[Bret] And so were cults.

146
00:07:47,589 --> 00:07:48,624
[bird squawks]

147
00:07:50,829 --> 00:07:54,067
[Bret] The cult that terrorized
Los Angeles in 1981,

148
00:07:54,068 --> 00:07:56,573
when I was a senior
in high school,

149
00:07:56,574 --> 00:08:01,683
was bloodthirsty, terrifying
and had similar habits
to The Trawler.

150
00:08:01,684 --> 00:08:02,852
[glass shatters]

151
00:08:02,853 --> 00:08:05,624
[Bret] In fact,

152
00:08:05,625 --> 00:08:07,829
law enforcement would suspect
that this cult was involved
in The Trawler murders

153
00:08:08,463 --> 00:08:12,037
and that the vicious ritualist
killings plaguing the city

154
00:08:12,038 --> 00:08:15,644
were done not by one member,
but by several.

155
00:08:15,645 --> 00:08:17,682
[♪ eerie music playing]

156
00:08:25,297 --> 00:08:28,070
[door creaks]

157
00:08:30,609 --> 00:08:32,778
[Bret] The city
had never truly recuperated

158
00:08:32,779 --> 00:08:35,050
from the Manson killings
a decade earlier.

159
00:08:35,619 --> 00:08:37,922
And like Charlie
and the Manson girls,

160
00:08:37,923 --> 00:08:41,328
the Riders of the Afterlife,
as they called themselves,

161
00:08:41,329 --> 00:08:43,734
reigned from Ojai
to the San Fernando Valley.

162
00:08:43,735 --> 00:08:46,773
[cult members murmuring,
screaming]

163
00:08:46,774 --> 00:08:48,677
[Bret] This group of young,

164
00:08:48,678 --> 00:08:51,950
barely-articulate hippies
formed out in the Mojave,

165
00:08:51,951 --> 00:08:55,123
acolytes of a leader
only referred to as Bruce,

166
00:08:55,124 --> 00:08:58,664
who turned out
to be a high school
English teacher from Lancaster,

167
00:08:58,665 --> 00:09:02,271
fired due
to sexual improprieties
with female students.

168
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:04,610
[exhales]

169
00:09:06,413 --> 00:09:10,053
[Bret] The cult did drugs,
acid, tranquilizers

170
00:09:10,054 --> 00:09:13,092
and their activities
quickly accelerated that year

171
00:09:13,093 --> 00:09:16,031
as Bruce became more addicted
and messianic.

172
00:09:16,032 --> 00:09:18,036
[screaming continues]

173
00:09:19,239 --> 00:09:21,710
Yeah.

174
00:09:21,711 --> 00:09:24,314
Well, maybe
I should ask for the recipe
so that I can make it for you.

175
00:09:24,315 --> 00:09:25,784
Honey?

176
00:09:25,785 --> 00:09:27,254
[♪ ominous music playing]

177
00:09:30,327 --> 00:09:31,630
[partner 1] Oh, my God.

178
00:09:32,298 --> 00:09:33,634
Is that blood?

179
00:09:35,237 --> 00:09:37,107
[partner 2] Go inside
and call the police.

180
00:09:38,376 --> 00:09:41,316
[Bret] Then came break-ins
in Hancock Park
and Silver Lake.

181
00:09:41,818 --> 00:09:44,121
They wanted to unify
through fear.

182
00:09:44,890 --> 00:09:47,227
- And then things got worse.
- [grunting]

183
00:09:47,228 --> 00:09:49,999
[Bret] The home invasions
escalated to violence.

184
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,470
The victims who survived
those early attacks

185
00:09:52,471 --> 00:09:56,279
said the intruder wore
a black ski mask and
matching jeans and turtleneck.

186
00:09:58,350 --> 00:10:00,420
He was
a terrifying shape-shifter.

187
00:10:06,399 --> 00:10:09,038
And then
the first murder occurred.

188
00:10:15,284 --> 00:10:16,754
[gasps]

189
00:10:17,523 --> 00:10:20,093
[news anchor 1]
...as authorities continue
to hunt for The Trawler,

190
00:10:20,094 --> 00:10:23,900
the serial killer
targeting young people
throughout Los Angeles.

191
00:10:23,901 --> 00:10:26,873
Police fear The Trawler
will strike again.

192
00:10:26,874 --> 00:10:31,148
Police are warning residents
throughout Los Angeles
to remain vigilant.

193
00:10:31,149 --> 00:10:35,356
A gruesome discovery
in Brentwood tonight after
a home invasion turned deadly.

194
00:10:35,357 --> 00:10:38,897
[Bret]
Between nightly news reports
of The Trawler murders,

195
00:10:38,898 --> 00:10:41,302
two other young people
were now missing,

196
00:10:41,303 --> 00:10:44,441
and then reports of the Riders
of the Afterlife attacks.

197
00:10:44,442 --> 00:10:47,248
The city was on edge
and so was I.

198
00:10:51,456 --> 00:10:54,997
Every night, I kept thinking
I heard someone in the house.

199
00:10:55,599 --> 00:10:57,802
I was at my desk working
on my novel,

200
00:10:58,236 --> 00:11:00,440
and whenever I stopped typing
I would feel a presence

201
00:11:00,441 --> 00:11:02,946
either lurking
outside the house

202
00:11:03,313 --> 00:11:04,949
or somewhere within it.

203
00:11:04,950 --> 00:11:06,720
[doorbell ringing]

204
00:11:11,196 --> 00:11:12,866
[doorbell continues ringing]

205
00:11:20,214 --> 00:11:22,283
[♪ eerie music playing]

206
00:11:22,284 --> 00:11:24,454
A number of times
I thought I heard the doorbell

207
00:11:24,455 --> 00:11:27,428
or the chiming that announced
the garage doors opening.

208
00:11:28,330 --> 00:11:31,837
For weeks I crept
through the house
clutching a weapon.

209
00:12:09,078 --> 00:12:10,347
But no one was there.

210
00:12:11,951 --> 00:12:13,053
Not yet.

211
00:12:16,092 --> 00:12:17,962
But I could tell
they were coming.

212
00:12:17,963 --> 00:12:19,398
[♪ sinister music plays]

213
00:13:02,318 --> 00:13:04,856
[sighs] Goddamn it, Liz.

214
00:13:05,457 --> 00:13:07,628
[grunts] Mom!

215
00:13:07,629 --> 00:13:09,231
[♪ "Déjà Vu" playing]

216
00:13:09,232 --> 00:13:10,300
Mom!

217
00:13:11,469 --> 00:13:14,008
♪ Long ago ♪

218
00:13:16,479 --> 00:13:19,118
♪ How can it be? ♪

219
00:13:21,222 --> 00:13:22,925
That's my Lancôme.

220
00:13:22,926 --> 00:13:25,029
You're not allowed
to wear eye shadow at school.

221
00:13:25,030 --> 00:13:26,298
I'm going out.

222
00:13:26,299 --> 00:13:27,635
I'm almost done.

223
00:13:27,636 --> 00:13:29,939
[Debbie breathes deeply]

224
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:32,511
I'm going to La Scala
with Joanie Carson
and Jackie Collins.

225
00:13:34,616 --> 00:13:37,220
Going out with Bret
tonight again? [huffs]

226
00:13:37,221 --> 00:13:39,191
Yes, Mother, we're in love.

227
00:13:39,660 --> 00:13:43,332
Being with each other
whenever you can is what being
in love looks like. Remember?

228
00:13:43,333 --> 00:13:46,271
You wanna look good
for your man. I get it.

229
00:13:46,272 --> 00:13:49,178
[breathes deeply] Oh, honey.

230
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:51,282
Men. [scoffs]

231
00:13:52,051 --> 00:13:55,323
All your meaningless
flirtations you think
will get you somewhere

232
00:13:55,324 --> 00:13:57,360
- and make you happy.
- Just give me the compact

233
00:13:57,361 --> 00:13:58,898
and have another Chablis, Mom.

234
00:14:02,104 --> 00:14:03,940
There's just so much
you don't know about them.

235
00:14:03,941 --> 00:14:05,510
There's just so much
you don't know.

236
00:14:05,511 --> 00:14:08,115
I know you're so cool
and so clever

237
00:14:08,116 --> 00:14:11,488
and so naive about
so many things.

238
00:14:11,489 --> 00:14:13,894
- You're so jealous.
- [scoffs] Jealous?

239
00:14:15,164 --> 00:14:17,569
Jealous of the trainer
at the stables?

240
00:14:18,170 --> 00:14:22,010
That dumb guy your father
cast in that absurd
roller-disco movie?

241
00:14:22,011 --> 00:14:23,346
And now Bret?

242
00:14:24,683 --> 00:14:27,488
- What's wrong with Bret?
- He's very aloof.

243
00:14:28,223 --> 00:14:30,527
And seemed more interested
in that book he's writing

244
00:14:30,528 --> 00:14:32,263
than coming
to his girlfriend's party

245
